    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2021-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Finalisation and presentation of the assessment for the implementation of e-PPGBM (MoH Nutrition Information System), in collaboration with UNICEF
- Continue providing inputs and support in the development and implementation of UN Data Strategy through leading the LNOB working group
- Finalisation of the UN perception survey, a work commissioned by UN RC Indonesia
- Continue working with Bappenas on identifying methodology to identify possible scenarios that can happen in MSME data landscape 
- Identify the needs of moH through data analytics to inform the arts of possible in a systematic process.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- e-PPGBM assessment has been finalised and report are being prepared for presentation in 2022
- Inputs and contribution to the UN Data Strategy has been rendered in a regular basis. LNOB working group has established a regular meeting
- The minimum viable product of MSME mapping has been produced and to be tested by Bappenas
- Research dive to identify strategy to integrate health data conducted for further deliberation</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Finalization and handover of MSME Analytical Dashboard to Bappenas
- Joint dissemination with the Ministry of Health for results of the Health Data Analytics for Managing Pandemics project
- Finalization and dissemination of Breaking Growth Barriers for Women Small and Medium Business Owners research with UN Women
- Finalization and dissemination of The Future of MSMEs in Indonesia horizon scanning exercise with Bappenas
- Finalization and dissemination of ePPGBM (Managing Nutrition Health Data Platform) implementation gap study with UNICEF and the Ministry of Health
- Institutional assessment and design of a low carbon analytics unit
- Explore scale-up of the analytical partnerships accelerator model into regional level initiative</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Public Institutions are capable of responsive, inclusive, participatory and representative decision-making 

This has been evidenced by the adoption of the Interactive COVID-19 vulnerability Map by the West Java Government and it has been made available for public use, also used as the reference for COVID-19 task force weekly update in West Java. To some extent, the tool has been used to help answer some policy questions related to COVID-19. 

Another example is the tool for digital diplomacy has been adopted and further used to generate data as a basis for monthly reporting to the Minister of Foreign Affairs.  

 

Output 4.3. Innovative technology solutions adopted for improved public service delivery (Strategic Plan Output 3.1.1) 

PLJ&#x2019;s work with West Java&#x2019;s provincial government has resulted in a tool that visualizes data relevant to COVID-19 response. In particular, the tool we developed utilized Facebook&#x2019;s population density data and combined it with other available data in Indonesia (including Susenas) to identify the risk of transmission and local capacity to handle it down to the village level. PLJ continues to work with the provincial government to identify specific pathways of how insights generated from the tool can feed into policy making processes and intervention design.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
